In the header comment of the shader code on that same page:

  "MIT License"

(The site's licence terms, https://godotshaders.com/license/, say that a submitted shader
is under the licence its author chose and that the code is the author's own or already
free to share. The shader's header names no other author and credits no other work. The
page's screenshot is not covered by the licence and is not used here.)

What this port changed
----------------------
The picture is made by the author's drop maths, ported to Metal Shading Language for
MacOS Live Wallpaper. See the note at the top of main.metal; in short:
 - The Godot wrapper (shader_type spatial, fragment(), UV, TIME, SCREEN_TEXTURE,
   SCREEN_UV, ALBEDO, NORMALMAP) was removed and the maths runs in mainImage. UV keeps
   Godot's orientation and is scaled by the screen height, so drops keep their shape on
   any screen shape.
 - The uniforms became constants at their default values, except dripping_speed, slowed
   from 1.0 to 0.17 so a streak takes about 24 seconds to cross the screen.
 - TIME is wrapped with fract(), which gives the same picture (it only enters through
   mod(., 1.0)) without losing precision after days of uptime.
 - The hard float(length(v) < r) tests became a one pixel wide coverage ramp
   (anti-aliasing). The unused PI constant was dropped.
 - Added by this port, not by the author: the engine's lighting and the screen texture
   are replaced by a fixed light, a Blinn-Phong highlight and a simple refraction driven
   by the author's normal (decoded the way Godot decodes NORMALMAP), over a procedural
   night-city bokeh background written for this port (it drifts and twinkles gently, and
   is sharper inside the drops), plus a tiny dither against banding. No image files are
   shipped; everything is computed.

